<description>Modify preview to use CreateANewOrganizationFormChild directly with exact session structure</description>
<content>
import React from 'react';
import { useParentState } from '../useIframeState';
import { CreateANewOrganizationFormChild } from '../../../../packages/features/ee/organizations/components/CreateANewOrganizationForm';
import { SessionProvider } from 'next-auth/react';
import { UserPermissionRole } from '@calcom/prisma/enums';

export default function ComponentPreview() {
  const [state, setState] = useParentState({
    mockSession: {
      type: 'boolean',
      value: true,
      label: 'Mock Session',
    },
  });

  // Create a session object that exactly matches what CreateANewOrganizationFormChild expects
  const session = {
    data: {
      user: {
        id: 1,
        name: 'Test User',
        email: 'user@example.com',
        username: 'testuser',
        role: UserPermissionRole.USER,
        organizationId: null,
        teams: [],
        locale: 'en',
      },
      expires: new Date(Date.now() + 2 * 86400).toISOString(),
    },
    status: "authenticated" as const,
    update: () => Promise.resolve(null),
  };

  if (!state.mockSession.value) {
    return null;
  }

  return (
    <div className="w-full">
      <SessionProvider session={session}>
        <CreateANewOrganizationFormChild 
          session={{
            data: session.data,
            status: "authenticated",
            update: session.update
          }} 
        />
      </SessionProvider>
    </div>
  );
}
</content>
</change>
</file>

Key changes made:
1. Imported CreateANewOrganizationFormChild directly instead of the main component
2. Created a session object that exactly matches the type expected by the child component
3. Passed the session directly to CreateANewOrganizationFormChild as a prop
4. Added proper type assertion for the status
5. Wrapped everything in SessionProvider for context
6. Added early return when mockSession is false
7. Made sure the session object structure exactly matches what's expected by the component

The main difference in this approach is that we're:
1. Using the child component directly
2. Passing the session as a prop rather than relying on the useSession hook
3. Ensuring the session object structure exactly matches what's expected

This should resolve the "Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'role')" error because we're now providing the session directly to the component that needs it, bypassing any potential context issues.

